    "case_summary": "A 74-year-old man with a history of multiple squamous cell carcinomas that were treated with Mohs micrographic surgery presented with a tender, enlarging scalp lesion of 6 months' duration.\n\nSkin examination revealed a 5-mm bright pink, dome-shaped papule with overlying vessels located at the crown of the scalp. There was no purulent discharge or overlying skin changes.\n\n{'Histopathology': {'Biopsy Findings': 'Hematoxylin-eosin staining demonstrated concentric proliferation of ovoid tumor cells present around small vessels over a hyalinized background stroma. The cells were hyperchromatic and variable in nuclear size, with scattered mitoses and necrosis.', 'Immunohistochemistry': '- Smooth muscle actin (SMA) was diffusely positive in the tumor cells\\n- CD31 immunostain highlighted many endothelial-lined vascular channels\\n- Negative staining for: smooth muscle myosin, h-caldesmon, calponin, desmin, Kaposi sarcoma–associated herpesvirus, SOX10, human melanoma black, S-100, and STAT6', 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Bright pink, dome-shaped papule of the scalp crown with overlying vessels measuring 5 mm', 'Figure B': 'Hematoxylin-eosin staining showing dermal-based tumor cells arranged in a vague nodular configuration', 'Figure C': 'Proliferation of tumor cells present around small vessels with scattered mitoses and necrosis', 'Figure D': 'Smooth muscle actin stain'}}}",
